# Uploads to Vercel.
#
# This file *replaces* .gitignore for uploads — Vercel does not consult both —
# so the `.env*` rules in .gitignore do not apply here. Without these lines
# `vercel deploy` uploads real environment files into the deployment source.
#
# Two separate problems, and the second is easy to miss: the credentials end up
# in the deployment, *and* Next loads an uploaded .env during the build, so
# local values silently win over the ones configured on the project — which is
# how a production build ends up pointing at a dev database.
#
# This deployment had no .vercelignore at all, and the working tree carries
# .env.local plus two credential backups (.env.local.bak-preSSO,
# .env.local.pre-rolefix). Every one of them was being uploaded.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build output and dependencies.
node_modules
.next
.turbo
dist

# The Expo app at the repository root. It has its own build and none of it is
# imported by the web application, so uploading it only makes the deployment
# larger.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, and that matters: `mobile/` on its own matches
# a directory of that name at *any* depth, which silently took `src/lib/mobile`
# with it — the geofence module `attendance.neon.ts` imports — and the build
# failed with module-not-found for a file that was sitting right there.
/mobile/
